Title: EP01 — Just Build It
Credit: Written by
Author: vibecode.town
Series: Vibe Coder Survival Guide Season 1
Source: Act 1-1 (I thought it worked) + Act 1-2 (Monday morning, everything broke)
Draft date: 2026-03-12
Contact: vibecode.town

# SEGMENT 1: HOOK [0:00-0:20]
# visual_type: sitcom
# characters: vee
# shorts_candidate: true

= Vee celebrates → explosion. Show the result first.

INT. VEE'S ROOM - NIGHT

Five monitors glow in a dark room. Vee leans back in her chair. Each screen shows code, dashboards, terminals — all running.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This is the moment every vibe coder lives for.

= Vee throws both arms up. Eyes wide. Huge grin. "BUILD SUCCESS" flashes green on every screen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You asked AI to build it. And it did. All of it.

= BEAT: 1 second of silence. One monitor flickers.

= One screen turns red. Then two. Then all five. Error messages cascade like a waterfall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then, three seconds later...

= Vee's smile disappears. She slowly lowers her mug.

= CU: Vee's face. Pure confusion. She can't read a single error.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
...it all falls apart.

> TITLE CARD: **JUST BUILD IT**

---

# SEGMENT 2: SITCOM ACT 1 [0:20-1:35]
# visual_type: sitcom
# characters: vee, bee
# shorts_candidate: false

= Three days earlier. Vee's vibe coding montage. Bee's first appearance.

INT. VEE'S ROOM - DAY (3 DAYS EARLIER)

> INSERT: "3 days earlier"

Bright room. Vee sits at her monitor. Terminal open.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Three days ago, Vee discovered something magical.

= Vee types. Files auto-generate on screen. Folders, code, packages — appearing from nothing.

= Vee's eyes go wide. She jumps out of her chair.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
What used to take three hours with ChatGPT took five minutes. You type what you want. AI builds it. Done.

= MONTAGE: Rapid cuts. Vee types → app appears. Types → app appears. Types → app appears. Each time faster. Each time her grin gets bigger.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A to-do app. Done.
A database. Done.
A login page. Done.

= Vee spins in her chair, arms crossed, nodding like a CEO. Five terminal windows run simultaneously.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
By day three, she had five agents running. She couldn't explain what each one did. But they ran. So she assumed they worked.

= On the desk, next to the monitor — a small bee sits on a pencil cup. BEE. Wings folded. Eyes closed. Completely unimpressed.

= Vee doesn't notice. She keeps typing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But there was someone who'd seen this story before.

= Bee opens one eye. Stares at Vee. Holds up a tiny sign: "WHAT ARE YOU BUILDING?"

= Vee finally notices. Freezes. Stares at the sign. Blinks.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She couldn't answer.

= Bee flips the sign. The back reads: "EXACTLY."

= Bee folds the sign, tucks it under one wing, and flies away. No drama. Just gone.

---

# SEGMENT 3: EXPLAINER [1:35-3:15]
# visual_type: explainer
# characters: []
# shorts_candidate: true

= Transition to abstract world. Data-driven explainer: what a spec is and why it matters.

> TRANSITION: Camera zooms into Vee's monitor → scrolling code fills the screen → code dissolves into data visualization

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ten thousand, eight hundred and forty-seven lines.

= A counter ticks up from 0 to 10,847. The number fills the screen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That's how much code Vee had after three days of vibe coding. Almost half of it — four thousand two hundred lines — was duplicated.

= The number splits. 6,647 stays white. 4,200 turns red and peels away into a pile.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Five AI agents built the same functions five different ways. Three date formatters. Two config parsers. Four input validators. None of them knew the others existed.

= Five stick-figure workers appear on a lot. Each builds independently. Five identical small houses rise side by side.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why? Because each AI session starts fresh. No memory. No shared context. Each one solves the problem from scratch — without checking if someone already solved it.

= The five houses wobble. One collapses into another. Chain reaction.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Industry data backs this up. Two-thirds of software projects fail. The number one reason? Incomplete requirements. In other words: nobody wrote down what we're building.

= Text overlay: "66% of IT projects fail — #1 cause: incomplete requirements" (Standish CHAOS Report)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And here's the worst part. A bug caught during planning costs one dollar to fix. The same bug caught in production? A hundred dollars.

= Simple bar chart animates: $1 → $10 → $100. The bars grow dramatically.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Barry Boehm proved this in 1981. Forty-five years later, it's still true. Especially when AI writes your code.

= The bar chart dissolves. New scene: a blank page appears.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So what IS a spec? It's not a hundred-page document. It's four questions.

= Four boxes draw themselves on the page, one by one:

NARRATOR (V.O.)
What am I trying to do? Why doesn't the current solution work? How should it work? What tools will I use?

= Text fills each box: "Purpose" / "Reason" / "Method" / "Means"

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Purpose. Reason. Method. Means. That's it. Answer those four, and you have a spec. Your frustration, organized — that IS the spec.

= The four boxes compress into a single sheet of paper. Text appears: "I want to use my home computer from anywhere."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
One sentence that explains everything you're building. Every decision flows from this.

> TRANSITION: The paper folds into a paper airplane → flies toward the camera → morphs into the paper on Vee's desk → camera pulls back

---

# SEGMENT 4: SITCOM ACT 2 [3:15-4:30]
# visual_type: sitcom
# characters: vee, bee
# shorts_candidate: false

= Vee alone. Stops coding. Writes frustrations on paper. Discovers what she's building.

INT. VEE'S ROOM - NIGHT (PRESENT)

Five monitors. Red errors. Vee's hands hover over the keyboard.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Back in the present. Ten thousand lines of code she can't read. And one more surprise.

= Vee scrolls through code. It doesn't end. She scrolls faster. Still doesn't end.

= She runs a security check. One result highlights in red: "Line 847 — shell injection."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Line eight forty-seven. An open door. Anyone could type a command into a text field and her computer would obey. That's not a bug. That's a welcome mat for strangers.

= Vee stares at the screen. Slowly pushes the chair away from the desk.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then she did something unusual.
She stopped coding.

= Vee opens a desk drawer. Pulls out paper and a pencil. Turns her back to the monitors.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She started writing. Not code. Just... what was bothering her.

= CU: Paper. Messy handwriting appears:
= "I don't know what I built."
= "There are five of them. They collect data. They organize. They analyze."

= The pencil stops.

= "But analyze WHAT?"

= Vee stares at the paper. One second.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And there it was. The question she'd been avoiding.

= The pencil moves again. Crosses out the old lines with big strokes.

= Writes fresh: "I want to use my home computer from anywhere."

= Below: "Files should stay in sync." "Access from any device." "Can't drop the connection."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Not features. Not tools.
The reason. The one sentence that explains everything.

= Vee picks up the paper. Turns back to the monitors.

= Red error messages. Before: an unreadable wall of text. Now —

= CU: One error line highlights itself: "Connection refused: sync agent cannot reach host."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The same errors. But now, one line makes sense.

= Vee's eyes track the line. Something clicks — shown through a slow blink, a slight tilt of the head.

= Corner of the frame: Bee sits on the edge of the desk. Watching. 0.5 seconds. Looks away.

---

# SEGMENT 5: ENDING [4:30-4:50]
# visual_type: sitcom
# characters: vee, bee
# shorts_candidate: true

= Mirror of HOOK. Same room, same errors, different person.

INT. VEE'S ROOM - NIGHT (CONTINUOUS)

= **Exact same composition as HOOK.** Five monitors. Red errors. Dark room.

Vee sits in her chair. The mug is in the same spot.

But Vee isn't panicking.

= She pins a piece of paper next to the monitor. It reads: "I want to use my home computer from anywhere."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Everything is still broken.

= BEAT.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But now she knows what to fix.

= Bee, corner of the frame. Tiny nod. 0.5 seconds. Back to deadpan.

> END CARD

NARRATOR (V.O.)
...at least, she thinks she does.

> NEXT: EP02 — "Fix One, Break Two"
